{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Data.Complex.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Fin","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Group.Finset.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Linarith"],"declarations":9,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.im_vanishes_on_deuteron_shapes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.im_vanishes_on_deuteron_shapes","kind":"theorem","line":190,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L190-L195","doc":"…yet `Im` still vanishes on every real shape, by the general theorem. This\nis the prune in miniature: an unconstrained conjugation-odd function,\nannihilated on exactly the locus the deuteron's triangulation lives on. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.im_i_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.im_i_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":187,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L187-L188","doc":"…and `Im` is nonzero somewhere, so the previous lemma is not vacuous. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.conjAntisymm_im","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.conjAntisymm_im","kind":"theorem","line":177,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L177-L185","doc":"A conjugation-odd function that is **not** identically zero: `Im`. It is\nnonzero at `i`, so the theorems above are not silently quantifying over only\nthe trivial function — they genuinely say that such a function, however large\noff the real axis, is pinned to `0` on it.\n\nThis is the honest form of the prune: `D` is not small on real shapes, it is\nzero, while being unconstrained elsewhere. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.conjAntisymm_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.conjAntisymm_zero","kind":"theorem","line":172,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L172-L175","doc":"The zero function is conjugation-odd, so the hypothesis is consistent. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.deuteron_volume_leg_eq_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.deuteron_volume_leg_eq_zero","kind":"theorem","line":150,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L150-L165","doc":"**The deuteron's volume leg is exactly zero.**\n\n`Vol = D(1/2) + D(2) = 0`, from conjugation antisymmetry alone — no `Li₂`\nevaluation, no numerics. This reproduces the `volume = 0.0` recorded in\n`deuteron-octahedral-gluing.witness.json`, and shows that figure is forced by\nthe shapes being real rather than being a numerical artefact of a degenerate\nsolve.\n\nConsequently an extended-Bloch **volume** term contributes nothing at `A = 2`,\nwhile the deuteron's binding is not zero. Any Bloch-group closure of the\nbinding magnitude must therefore come from the `CS` leg, or from a different\nknot assignment — not from `Vol` on this triangulation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.deuteronShapes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.deuteronShapes","kind":"def","line":145,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L145-L148","doc":"The two shapes SnapPy reports for the `σ₁³` closure, as reals. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.sum_eq_zero_of_real_shapes_forall_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.sum_eq_zero_of_real_shapes_forall_card","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L134-L139","doc":"The count-independence, stated so it cannot be missed: for **every** `n`,\na triangulation by `n` real-shaped ideal tetrahedra has volume leg `0`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.sum_eq_zero_of_real_shapes","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.sum_eq_zero_of_real_shapes","kind":"theorem","line":118,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L118-L132","doc":"**The volume leg vanishes on any flat triangulation, at any tetrahedron\ncount.**\n\nIf every shape parameter is real then `∑ D(zᵢ) = 0`, whatever the index set.\n\nThe count-independence is the point. An extensive term is one that grows with\nthe number of constituents; this sum does not grow, because every summand is\nindividually zero. So the volume leg cannot be made extensive by refining the\ntriangulation or by moving to a heavier nucleus whose closure is still\nflat. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.eq_zero_of_conjAntisymm_ofReal","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MassTheory.BlochVolumeLeg.eq_zero_of_conjAntisymm_ofReal","kind":"theorem","line":103,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MassTheory/BlochVolumeLegFlatVanishing.lean#L103-L116","doc":"**A conjugation-odd real-valued function vanishes on the reals.**\n\n`D(x̄) = −D(x)` and `x̄ = x` for real `x`, so `D(x) = −D(x)`.\n\nThis is the whole mechanism. Bloch–Wigner's vanishing on the real line is\nusually quoted as a computation with `Li₂`; it is not one, it is this\ntwo-line symmetry argument, and stating it that way is what makes it\napplicable without a dilogarithm in the library. ","signatureChunk":0}]}